```python
class User:
    def __init__(self, name):
        if not name.strip():
            raise ValueError("name cannot be empty")
        self.name = name

    def rename(self, new_name):
        self.name = new_name

user = User("Mina")
user.rename("Meera")
print(user.name)
```

The call supplies only the visible argument. Python passes the new `User` as `self` to `__init__` and later passes it to `rename`.

```python
class UpperName(str):
    def __new__(cls, value):
        cleaned = value.strip().upper()
        return super().__new__(cls, cleaned)

    def __init__(self, value):
        self.original_length = len(value)

name = UpperName("  Ada  ")
print(name)
print(name.original_length)
```

`__new__` decides the immutable string value. `__init__` can still attach separate instance metadata afterward.
